I think you are always going to have trouble fitting any model when you 
only have four studies. You are estimating quite a few parameters. Sorry 
to be the bearer of bad news.

Michael

On 27/09/2026 04:08, Donghui Kim via R-sig-meta-analysis wrote:
> Dear list,
> 
> I am fitting the bivariate binomial-normal model for diagnostic test
> accuracy (Chu & Cole 2006) with lme4 in a subset analysis that has only
> four studies. The code below uses made-up counts that reproduce the
> behaviour I see, including one small study with no false positives.
> 
> The full model is singular with the correlation at -1; the specificity
> SD there is not zero, but it is tied to sensitivity through that
> correlation. I then followed the usual fallback and fixed the
> correlation at 0 (which here is the same as fitting separate univariate
> random-effects models). In that fit the between-study variance for
> specificity is estimated at exactly zero, so the 95% prediction interval
> for specificity, computed as b +/- 1.96 * sqrt(tau^2 + var(b)) on the
> logit scale, collapses onto the confidence interval, while sensitivity
> keeps a wider prediction interval.
> 
> I have read the 2019 thread "convergence in Mada" on this list, which
> notes that few studies and a perfect between-study correlation can
> cause problems and suggests profiling the correlation. I have also read
> Takwoingi et al. (2017), who consider univariate random-effects models
> appropriate when the bivariate model cannot be fitted, and univariate
> fixed-effect models when there is minimal or no variability (their
> Box 1). In the real data I profiled the correlation: on a grid of 11
> values from -0.999 to 0.999, the change in deviance is at most 0.075,
> so the data carry essentially no information about it.
> 
> My question is about what comes after that advice. With k = 4 and
> tau^2 estimated at 0 for one arm, what is the most defensible way to
> report that arm's prediction interval? The options I can see are
> (a) the normal-quantile interval above, identical to the confidence
> interval; (b) a t quantile on k - 2 degrees of freedom (Higgins et al.
> 2009), which with tau^2 = 0 is only a widened confidence interval, not
> a statement about heterogeneity; (c) saying that the prediction
> interval is not estimable for that arm; (d) a weakly informative prior
> on tau, for example with blme; or (e) reporting an upper
> profile-likelihood limit for tau instead, since tau^2 = 0 at k = 4 is
> not evidence of homogeneity.
> 
> library(lme4)
> # made-up counts; study s4 is small and has no false positives
> d <- data.frame(study = paste0("s", 1:4),
> TP = c(42, 36, 8, 10), FN = c(10, 16, 8, 18),
> TN = c(111, 93, 80, 12), FP = c(39, 28, 20, 0))
> long <- rbind(
> data.frame(study = d$study, grp = "sens", succ = d$TP, fail = d$FN),
> data.frame(study = d$study, grp = "spec", succ = d$TN, fail = d$FP))
> long$grp <- factor(long$grp)
> ctrl <- glmerControl(optimizer = "bobyqa",
> optCtrl = list(maxfun = 2e5))
> 
> m1 <- glmer(cbind(succ, fail) ~ 0 + grp + (0 + grp | study),
> data = long, family = binomial, control = ctrl)
> isSingular(m1); VarCorr(m1) # TRUE; corr = -1
> 
> long$is_sens <- as.numeric(long$grp == "sens")
> long$is_spec <- as.numeric(long$grp == "spec")
> m2 <- glmer(cbind(succ, fail) ~ 0 + grp + (0 + is_sens | study) +
> (0 + is_spec | study),
> data = long, family = binomial, control = ctrl)
> isSingular(m2); VarCorr(m2) # TRUE; SD(is_spec) = 0
> 
> b <- fixef(m2); vb <- diag(vcov(m2)); V <- as.data.frame(VarCorr(m2))
> t2 <- c(V$vcov[V$grp == "study" & V$var1 == "is_sens"],
> V$vcov[V$grp == "study.1" & V$var1 == "is_spec"])
> ci <- cbind(ci.lo = b - 1.96 * sqrt(vb),
> ci.hi = b + 1.96 * sqrt(vb))
> pred <- cbind(pi.lo = b - 1.96 * sqrt(t2 + vb),
> pi.hi = b + 1.96 * sqrt(t2 + vb))
> round(plogis(cbind(est = b, ci, pred)), 3)
> # est ci.lo ci.hi pi.lo pi.hi
> # grpsens 0.614 0.423 0.775 0.250 0.883
> # grpspec 0.773 0.728 0.812 0.728 0.812
> # (R 4.6.0, lme4 2.0-6)
> 
> Thank you for any advice.
